INT. TERMINATOR GREENHOUSE - TIDALLY LOCKED WORLD - TWILIGHT

A long glass nave on the border between day and night. To the east, a white-hot sun hangs unmoving. To the west, black sky and frozen plains. Inside, tomato vines climb silver wires, heavy with fruit.

ILSA VANTONGEREN, 50s, botanist, hair wrapped in a faded scarf, kneels among the roots. She hums as she works, one hand cupping a green fruit like a held breath.

BRAM, 20s, her apprentice, stands behind her. He holds a tablet and stares at it as if it had insulted him.

BRAM
Ilsa. The heat sink is at ninety-four.

ILSA
Ninety-four of what?

BRAM
Percent.

She rises slowly. Her knees crack. She doesn't look at the tablet. She looks at the glass.

ILSA
It was ninety-one an hour ago.

BRAM
I know.

ILSA
Then why are you telling me like it's news?

He swallows. He pockets the tablet.

QUILLON, 30s, the colony's engineer, jogs down the nave, boots clanging on the grating. He is out of breath, face slick, goggles pushed up on his forehead.

QUILLON
Ilsa! Bram! The shade array's jammed.

ILSA
Jammed how?

QUILLON
It won't rotate. The motor's seized.

BRAM
Because of the heat?

QUILLON
Because of a bolt. Someone put a bolt in the gear.

ILSA
Someone?

QUILLON
Or something.

Dr. Sephora Oyama's voice crackles from a speaker in the roof.

SEPHORA (V.O.)
Greenhouse, this is Medical. We have a patient with heat stroke in the corridor. Is anyone free to--

ILSA
(cutting in)
We're busy.

BRAM
(over her)
Send Quillon!

QUILLON
(over Bram)
I can't, I'm needed here!

SEPHORA (V.O.)
Please. She's asking for you, Ilsa.

Ilsa stops. Her hand falls from the tomato. The vines rustle, as if the plants were leaning to listen.

ILSA
Who is?

SEPHORA (V.O.)
Your daughter.

She turns to the glass. Stares at the white sun. Her shoulders fall, then straighten.

ILSA
Bram. Take the tomatoes. All of them. Into the cold room.

BRAM
All of them?

ILSA
All. Quillon, fix the array. I don't care if you have to swing from it.

QUILLON
And you?

ILSA
I'm going to my daughter.

She is already running. Her scarf comes loose and trails behind her like a flag.

Bram watches her go. Quillon grabs his arm.

QUILLON (CONT'D)
Come on. We've got maybe ten minutes.

BRAM
Before what?

QUILLON
Before the glass starts cracking.

He is already moving. Bram scrambles after, arms full of fruit, tomatoes bouncing and rolling across the floor.
